  "query_preview": "..Very strong high pressure remains over Arizona, with \na gradual shift to the north over the next few days. Dry air aloft \ncontinues to inhibit convective development. Most of the lingering \nmoisture remains across southern Arizona up into portions of central \nArizona. As a result, expect isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms across the higher terrain, mainly focused over eastern \nArizona through Tuesday. Storm activity remains diurnally driven \nwith showers and storms dissipating as we head into the evening each \nday. \n\nHeat remains the main story through the middle of the week with near-\nrecord to record temperatures across parts of the area. Extreme Heat \nWarnings continue over the Grand Canyon and Glen and Marble Canyons \nthrough Wednesday and Heat Advisories remain in place through \nTuesday across much of northeastern Arizona. \n\nAs the high starts to push to the north, we will see an increase in \nmoisture and temperatures will start to cool. The placement of the \nhigh looks to remain in a more favorable position for the region to \nallow for the monsoon to ramp up as we head into the end of the \nweek. Expect daily chances for isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms each day, especially across the higher terrain. As the \nmodels have come into better alignment on the position of the high, \nconfidence is increasing in the more active monsoon returning. With \ntemperatures across the Grand Canyon hovering around warning \ncriteria for the second half of the week, we may need to extend the \ncurrent heat headline beyond Wednesday but that may dependent on \njust how quickly the moisture pushes north. For now, expect a more \nactive pattern to return with some slightly cooler temperatures.",
